<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Ch04_P02_Proof_line_by_line</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Here is the proof from the example above, in numbered lines.</p>
<table>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Let \(n\) be an odd number.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Then \(n = 2k + 1\) for some integer \(k\).</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>So \(n^2 - 1 = (2k + 1)^2 - 1\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>\(= 4k^2 + 4k + 1 - 1\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>\(= 4k^2 + 4k\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>\(= 4k(k + 1)\).</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>Since \(k\) and \(k + 1\) are consecutive integers, one of them is even,</td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td>so \(k(k + 1)\) is even.</td></tr>
<tr><td>9</td><td>So \(k(k + 1) = 2m\) for some integer \(m\).</td></tr>
<tr><td>10</td><td>So \(n^2 - 1 = 4 \times 2m = 8m\),</td></tr>
<tr><td>11</td><td>which is divisible by 8.</td></tr>
<tr><td>12</td><td>So \(n^2 - 1\) is divisible by 8 for every odd number \(n\).</td></tr>
</table>
<p>(a) In which line is the definition of an odd number used?</p>
<div>Line [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div>
<p>(b) In which line is the fact that \(k(k + 1)\) is even used?</p>
<div>Line [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>
<p>(c) Lines 1 to 11 are about one odd number, \(n\). Line 12 is about every odd number. Which line makes this last step allowed?</p>
<div>Line [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Each line of the proof makes one step, and each step has one reason.</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Line 1 is the hypothesis. The definition of odd turns it into line 2, so (a) is line 2.</p></li>
<li><p>Lines 3 to 6 are algebra. Line 7 uses the fact that \(k\) is an integer (line 2), and line 8 follows from line 7. Line 9 uses the fact that \(k(k + 1)\) is even: by the definition of even, it is \(2m\) for some integer \(m\). So (b) is line 9. Line 10 then puts lines 6 and 9 together.</p></li>
<li><p>Line 1 lets \(n\) be any odd number, and nothing else about \(n\) is used. So the same argument works for every odd number, and the conclusion in line 12 may say ‘every’: (c) is line 1.</p></li>
</ul>]]></text></generalfeedback>
